**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), bonding and solid-state sections; [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), bonding and network-structure lectures; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), atomic-scale structure and anisotropy.

Diamond and graphite illustrate why elemental identity does not specify a unique material response. Their bonding arrangements and connectivity differ. Graphite's layered structure invites directional questions; a single-layer, finite-flake or defective specimen is not identical to bulk graphite in every measurement.

Distinguish structure, morphology and manufacturing history when comparing fibres, powders, films and bulk pieces. Surface chemistry, interfaces, orientation and porosity can dominate the behavior of a manufactured carbon product. A remarkable value measured on one carefully prepared specimen is not automatically a design value for a larger assembly.

**Work:** Compare two carbon forms through a drawn bonding network and one direction-dependent observation. Identify the specimen scale and method behind a published property. Explain which structural change would be needed for the proposed transformation and why an equilibrium preference alone would not establish its rate.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
